<title>DUBAI: New Islamic bank set up in Yemen.</title>
<headline>New Islamic bank set up in Yemen.</headline>
<dateline>DUBAI 1996-11-14</dateline>
<text>
<p>A new Islamic bank has been set up in Yemen with a capital of one billion rials ($10 million), its general manager Algharib Nasser was on Thursday quoted as saying.</p>
<p>He told the London-based al-Hayat newspaper that the Jeddah-based Islamic Development Bank, Jordan Islamic Bank, the Saudi Dallah Albaraka group and Qatar International Islamic Bank owned 25 percent of the new bank -- the Yemeni Islamic Bank for Finance and Investment.</p>
<p>The rest was owned by Yemeni investors, he said.</p>
<p>Islamic banks do not deal in interest -- the core of the Western banking system -- as it is regarded by many Moslems as usury, which is banned under Islamic sharia law.</p>
</text>
